@@ -1242,6 +1242,28 @@ Every balance call in this file is checked by simulation, and CI plays seeded ro
 
 **Tried and not adopted: an approximate board split.** A rougher territory search was 1.38 times faster, but 6.7% of positions went another way, and play shifted. A checked version saved only 1.07–1.15 times.
 
+## A first round that teaches
+
+The owner, after playing: the lessons flashed past "too quick to really register", sometimes "in your face in the middle of the screen" on a phone, while you're steering a fast game; and "can dialogue bubbles be used to teach you? … show players what they can do and shouldn't do, rather than flash them in the eyes with signs." Measured (the teaching audit, 18 rounds as a new player): a banner was up 39–50% of play, one every 5–7 s, lessons chained back to back (seven in 18 s as sudden death began), 47% of phone lessons sat within two cells of your head, and the lessons about your own head (the choke, the cough) were told a median 13–16 cells away from it. These are the calls.
+
+**Your snake teaches you.** The rules that happen to you are said by your own snake, in a speech bubble by your head, where your eyes already are: one short idea, at most 32 characters, in the first person, saying what the rivals say for the same moment ("Ptooey! That’s me!" against Gobbler's "Ptooey! That one’s ME!"), so you and the rivals meet the same rules the same way. Its bubble is the rivals' bubble (beside your head going up or down, above it going sideways, never over your path, gliding), in your green, a touch larger, placed first (the rivals' bubbles make way), on the side the YOU arrow isn't, and never faded for your head (it's there on purpose). It shows with the chatter off (it's a lesson, not chatter), stays out of the log, and goes to the screen reader.
+
+**What you can do is shown, not told.** A move is taught by a picture on the cells it's about, drawn by your head while your snake says how, with what to press drawn in its bubble as what you'd press (a key cap, or a hand tapping or swiping): the jump as sky dots arching from your head to a ring where you'd land (round six B's own landing ring), faint until it's near enough that a press would go; the flash as ember speed lines to a ghost of your head two cells on; a turn as a dashed arrow curving off your head (once YOU has gone). One picture at a time; the bubbles keep off it; with reduced motion nothing in it moves.
+
+**A first round that teaches, one thing at a time.** What you've learned is kept on this device (`snake-taught`: the moves you've done, the rules you've met, the rounds you've played). Your first round has one rival fewer (never a softer one: no rival plays differently against you) and no Go! banner, and teaches the moves in turn, each waiting until you've done it:
+1. **Steer**, from Go: "Swipe, and I’ll turn!" (a hand swiping) or "[↓] and I’ll turn!" until you steer.
+2. **Jump**, the first time something to hop is on your line (a rival's body, a hole, fire) with somewhere to land and nobody's head close: "Tap, and I’ll hop it!" or "[Space] and I’ll hop it!" with the arch. Done: "Hup! That cost my tail tip."
+3. **Flash**, at a calm moment (no rival head within 5 cells, the way clear), given the jump's moment first for 10 s: "Swipe ahead: I’ll flash!" or "[E] and I’ll flash!" with the ghost. Done: "Zip! Two cells in a blink."
+A prompt never nags or flickers: its words stay up at least 3.5 s once said (up to 8 s while the moment lasts), come again only 12 s later and at most four times a round; its picture shows at each moment, a new one no sooner than 2 s after the last, and one left unheeded for 10 s rests 10 s more. A move not done comes back next round. 2.5 s pass between one move and the next, and a remark (below) always comes first.
+
+**Round six B and C teach the same way.** A new move is taught the first time it could happen to you, by its own cue drawn as the picture and your snake saying what to press: the island hop (the landing ring three cells on: "Tap: I’ll hop to that island!"), the golden flight (the gold ring that follows you: "Hold, and I’ll fly! Steer me!"), a cut (the slash of light and the beads it would spill: "Swipe ahead: I’ll cut it!"), dropping your tail (the same across your own body: "Swipe ahead: I’ll drop my tail!"), a bridge (its planks lit and its tail end creaking: "A bridge, till its tail passes!"). A rule that happens to you gets your snake's remark once ("Bonk! Bumped mid-air!", "Ouch! Too thick! Cost me five."). The woodpecker's acts are everyone's news, so each gets one calm banner, and its gaze is drawn on the cells it watches, for everyone, since on a phone its eye is too small to read.
+
+**Later rounds teach only what's new.** A rule's remark comes the first time it happens to you, ever, not every round: a crash ("Ow! That cost me tail."), a choke, the cough, hunger, fire, a golden pickup, a cut, a peck under you, a dig near you, a press that couldn't go. The last-life warnings come every round ("One more crash and I’m out!", "Starving, and tiny! Food!"). After that the board's own cues say it every time (the beads a crash knocks off, the puff of a choke, flames, the gold frame, your tag's bar and the rumble). The Field guide stays the full reference.
+
+**Banners are for the round's news**: the woodpecker, first blood, the last two, the weather, a spawn, the last apples. They come in calmly (no slam from 1.9×, no red throb), at the top or the board's foot, never the middle (on a phone held upright that's the screen's dead centre), and never straight after one another: routine news within `BANNER_GAP_MS` (6 s) of the last banner is dropped.
+
+**The woodpecker, bigger on a phone.** Held upright, it perched on the ledge under the board at whatever size the ledge allowed: 52 px tall on a 390×844 phone and 38 px on a 360×640 (against 136 on a 1920 desktop), its eye 3–4 px. Now the ledge reaches on down over the chatter's head row, whose middle is empty, so the bird stands there at full size, as on every screen: 4.9 cells, about a fifth of the board (78 px at 390×844, 72 at 360×640, its eye 5–6 px). It stands in front of the moss, keeps off the chatter's label and switch (taps pass through it), and still covers no cell. Watching (the speed buttons under the ledge), it's the ledge alone, as before. On its side, a phone already had it at full size in the gutter.
+
 ## Phones
 
 The owner, on a phone: playable, but not optimal, with space wasted on the border; on its side, "the play area becomes miniscule"; where would the woodpecker go with a bigger board, since it perches outside it; and could the screen stay awake while you play or watch. These are the calls, as built. Every number is CSS px, measured in headless Chrome by `tools/layout-test.js`, which checks all of this on 24 screens and at every height in between held upright.
